(i) Structure A is a **plasmid**, a circular, extra-chromosomal DNA of bacteria. Structure B is a **bacteriophage**, a virus that infects bacteria.

(ii) Both are used as **cloning vectors** in biotechnology:
- they can carry a foreign gene (the gene of interest) into the host cell;
- they replicate within bacterial cells independently of the control of the chromosomal DNA;
- plasmids normally have a few to many copies per cell, and bacteriophages have a very high number per cell, so the linked foreign DNA is multiplied to the same copy number;
- a plasmid vector also carries selectable markers, such as antibiotic resistance genes, which help pick out the transformants.

(a) Characteristics of stem cells:
- They are capable of **self-renewal**: they can divide repeatedly to produce more stem cells.
- They are undifferentiated and have the potency to **differentiate** into any kind of cell, tissue or organ of the body.

(b) Stem cells can be obtained from the **inner cell mass of the blastocyst** (embryonic stem cells), from **umbilical cord blood** and from the **bone marrow** of adults.

(c) Any two applications:
- treatment of diabetes, by forming new insulin-producing islets of Langerhans;
- restoring vision, by injecting stem cells to repair the damaged eye tissue;
- treating disorders of the blood and immune system, such as leukaemia, by bone marrow (stem cell) transplant;
- repairing tissue lost in spinal cord injuries or heart disease.
